WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:17.255
What are these toy soldiers?"

2
00:00:18.055 --> 00:00:21.156
Laurie was looking through an archaeology book in Grandpa's study.

3
00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:25.658
"Those aren't toys, Laurie," Grandpa said, looking at the picture.

4
00:00:26.218 --> 00:00:29.700
"Those are life-size clay soldiers!

5
00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:36.410
And they're on duty for eternity, guarding the tomb of the first emperor of one of the oldest

6
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.930
civilizations in the world—China!

7
00:00:39.731 --> 00:00:41.011
Take a look at the next photo."

8
00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:47.953
Laurie turned the page and saw an archaeologist carefully dusting the top of one clay soldier's head.

9
00:00:49.053 --> 00:00:52.434
The archaeologist and the statue were nearly the same size!

10
00:00:52.454 --> 00:00:54.875
"They are big!"

11
00:00:55.415 --> 00:00:56.215
she exclaimed.

12
00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:58.676
"And there are so many of them!

13
00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.573
Who was this emperor?

14
00:01:01.654 --> 00:01:06.377
He must have been really important to have all these soldiers made just for him."

15
00:01:06.397 --> 00:01:11.020
"Well, as emperor, you can pretty much get whatever you want.

16
00:01:12.321 --> 00:01:14.162
But, yes, he was important.

17
00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:22.828
Known as Qin Shihuangdi, he was the first emperor of China and the emperor of the Qin dynasty,

18
00:01:23.228 --> 00:01:25.049
which lasted from 221 BC to 207 BC.

19
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:35.704
In fact, the romanized name ‘China’ is believed to come from the Qin dynasty.

20
00:01:36.385 --> 00:01:44.930
Chinese civilization had been around since about 1600 BC, but Shihuangdi was the first ruler to unite the

21
00:01:44.991 --> 00:01:48.193
land into an empire ruled by one government.

22
00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:52.796
He was a cruel leader, but he did a lot for China.

23
00:01:54.324 --> 00:02:02.266
He ordered that the same measuring, writing, and money systems be used throughout the empire, which helped trade.

24
00:02:03.327 --> 00:02:11.329
He also organized the building of many roads, canals, and the longest structure ever built—the Great Wall of

25
00:02:11.429 --> 00:02:13.929
China." "How was he cruel?"

26
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:15.110
Laurie asked.

27
00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:22.872
"Building these enormous projects, especially the Great Wall, required a lot of hard work.

28
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.163
Shihuangdi forced his people to work for him.

29
00:02:28.204 --> 00:02:31.726
They often worked long hours on difficult jobs.

30
00:02:32.907 --> 00:02:36.270
Many actually died during the building of the Great Wall.

31
00:02:37.251 --> 00:02:43.616
He also believed that in order to rule a large empire, a ruler must make strict laws.

32
00:02:44.757 --> 00:02:51.563
One law punished scholars who thought or wrote about things the emperor believed were harmful or not useful

33
00:02:51.623 --> 00:02:52.404
to his empire.

34
00:02:53.564 --> 00:02:58.928
These included literature, philosophy, and of course criticism of his rule.

35
00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:07.174
He also famously burned all history books because he thought they might make people question his authority.

36
00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:08.315
Can you believe it?"

37
00:03:08.335 --> 00:03:13.339
"Wow. Without history books how would we learn about the past?"

38
00:03:13.779 --> 00:03:14.539
Laurie asked.

39
00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:20.544
"Well, written history helps, but there are other ways of discovering the past.

40
00:03:22.012 --> 00:03:23.834
Archaeology, for example!

41
00:03:24.494 --> 00:03:30.158
Luckily for Chinese historians, Shihuangdi's style of rule didn't last long.

42
00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:39.545
Soon after his death in 210 BC, his empire collapsed and the Han dynasty began.

43
00:03:40.626 --> 00:03:44.629
Han rulers looked to China's past for ideas about the future.

44
00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.432
They returned to the ideas of Confucius.

45
00:03:50.172 --> 00:03:57.457
He was an ancient scholar who believed that rulers should lead by good example only— not by forcing

46
00:03:57.497 --> 00:04:02.100
people to obey strict laws— and that people should respect their elders.

47
00:04:03.161 --> 00:04:10.866
His philosophy later spread throughout Asia, and his influence on Asian culture and society can still be seen

48
00:04:10.926 --> 00:04:16.830
today. Unlike the first emperor, Han rulers prized scholars.

49
00:04:18.571 --> 00:04:25.191
In fact, during the Han dynasty, a system was created to make sure that only the best scholars

50
00:04:25.271 --> 00:04:26.812
would receive government jobs.

51
00:04:27.853 --> 00:04:32.935
Anyone who wanted to work for the government had to take an extremely difficult test.

52
00:04:34.076 --> 00:04:37.438
China had the first government full of scholars!

53
00:04:38.438 --> 00:04:46.743
And nearly two thousand years later, this practice of making government employees pass tests was adopted by American

54
00:04:46.783 --> 00:04:48.484
and European governments too.

55
00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:59.593
The Han rulers continued to worry about foreign invaders, however, so they kept building Shihuangdi's Great Wall and

56
00:04:59.673 --> 00:05:02.555
created more roads for soldiers to travel on.

57
00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.638
With improved roads, trade continued to grow.

58
00:05:07.678 --> 00:05:15.082
The famous Silk Road, one of the greatest international trading routes in history, developed during the Han dynasty.

59
00:05:16.163 --> 00:05:23.919
Goods such as silk, tea, pottery, and spices traveled out of China to India, the Middle East, Russia,

60
00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:25.520
and Europe along that route.

61
00:05:26.981 --> 00:05:32.704
The exchange of ideas and culture between the East and the West also began along the Silk Road."

62
00:05:32.724 --> 00:05:35.065
"Was the road made out of silk?"

63
00:05:35.485 --> 00:05:36.246
Laurie asked.

64
00:05:37.286 --> 00:05:38.207
Grandpa chuckled.

65
00:05:38.227 --> 00:05:43.510
"No, but a lot of silk traveled along it.

66
00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.172
Silk, you see, was invented by the Chinese.

67
00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:55.867
Evidence shows they were making it as early as 1300 BC, but no one outside of China knew

68
00:05:55.927 --> 00:05:58.109
about it until centuries later.

69
00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:05.935
Once people of other countries touched the beautiful fabric, they loved it and wanted it for themselves.

70
00:06:06.916 --> 00:06:11.160
But the Chinese were determined to keep their silk-making process a secret.

71
00:06:12.241 --> 00:06:15.524
If foreigners wanted silk, they'd have to pay or trade.

72
00:06:16.564 --> 00:06:20.027
So silk was carried out of China along the Silk Road.

73
00:06:21.749 --> 00:06:26.913
Actually many things that were invented in ancient China would later travel to the West.

74
00:06:28.635 --> 00:06:29.976
There's paper, for one.

75
00:06:30.416 --> 00:06:34.940
And the wheelbarrow, the compass, and the first machine to detect earthquakes!

76
00:06:36.021 --> 00:06:43.788
The Han dynasty valued knowledge and learning, and they encouraged scientists to study and develop new things.

77
00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.304
They also valued the arts.

78
00:06:46.805 --> 00:06:52.286
The Han dynasty is known for its beautiful and unique pottery, poetry, and sculpture.

79
00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:57.147
Chinese civilization really flourished during this period.

80
00:06:59.008 --> 00:07:06.850
The dynasty lasted longer than any other—about four hundred years— and the Chinese consider it so important that

81
00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:10.551
even today they call themselves people of the Han."

82
00:07:10.571 --> 00:07:14.192
"Laurie?" Mom suddenly called.

83
00:07:14.668 --> 00:07:16.729
"Have you seen my red silk blouse?

84
00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.291
I want to wear it when I go out tonight."

85
00:07:19.311 --> 00:07:23.213
"You mean your Chinese red silk blouse?"

86
00:07:23.653 --> 00:07:24.734
Laurie shouted back.

87
00:07:24.754 --> 00:07:29.016
"No, I don't have a Chinese blouse, Laurie."

88
00:07:29.557 --> 00:07:31.958
Now Mom's voice was coming from outside the door.

89
00:07:31.978 --> 00:07:34.900
"What are you guys talking about in there anyway?"

90
00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.981
"Oh, all kinds of things!

91
00:07:37.641 --> 00:07:40.423
Silk, clay soldiers, a great wall!"

92
00:07:40.803 --> 00:07:41.644
Laurie answered.

93
00:07:42.292 --> 00:07:44.775
She turned to Grandpa and handed him back his book.

94
00:07:44.795 --> 00:07:47.338
"I'd better go help Mom find her blouse.

95
00:07:47.799 --> 00:07:49.020
Thanks again, Grandpa.

96
00:07:49.461 --> 00:07:51.824
See you in another place at another time!"
